# CLAUDE.md

This file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.

@AGENTS.md

## Project overview

Swifty Companion — a 42 school project. A two-screen mobile app: search a 42 intra login, then display that user's profile (profile picture, contact/level details, skills with percentages, completed and failed projects), authenticating against the 42 intra API via OAuth2. Built with Expo (React Native + TypeScript, Expo Router, SDK 54), targeting iOS via Expo Go / a dev client on a physical iPhone.

## Working mode — read before touching any code

The developer is using this project to learn React Native and TypeScript, and wants to write the implementation themselves. **Do not write feature/implementation code unless explicitly asked to.** Default mode is tutor/reviewer:
- Give small, scoped exercises (one concept at a time) rather than full screens.
- When shown code, point out bugs and non-idiomatic patterns and explain *why*, but let the developer write the fix.
- Ask comprehension-checking questions rather than lecturing.
- Only write code directly when explicitly asked for an implementation (as opposed to an exercise or a review).

## Commands

- `npx expo start` — start the Metro bundler (scan QR with Expo Go on a physical device for the target platform)
- `npx expo start --web` — web target, useful only for a quick visual sanity check (react-native-web is present); not a substitute for testing on the real target platform
- `npx tsc --noEmit` — typecheck. Note: `app-example/` is a stale directory generated by `npm run reset-project` (git-ignored, not part of the app) — filter its errors out when reading output, e.g. `npx tsc --noEmit | grep -v '^app-example'`
- `npx expo lint` — ESLint via the `eslint-config-expo` flat config

## Architecture

- **Expo Router** (file-based routing): routes live under `app/`. `app/_layout.tsx` defines the root `<Stack>`.
- Path alias `@/*` resolves to the project root (`tsconfig.json`), e.g. `@/components/Foo`.
- TypeScript strict mode is enabled.
- `npm run reset-project` (from the default Expo template) has already been run once, moving the original starter code into `app-example/` (git-ignored) and leaving `app/` minimal. Don't run it again without checking with the user first.

### Route structure (non-obvious naming)

- `app/index.tsx` (route `/`) — the **login screen** (OAuth2 flow entry point), not a landing/home screen. Also runs a mount-time check (`tokenStillValid()`) to skip straight to `/search` if a valid session already exists.
- `app/search.tsx` (route `/search`) — the **login-search screen** (was the original `index.tsx` before the login screen took over `/`; renamed to make room). Requires a valid token to load — redirects back to `/` if missing/expired.
- `app/profile/[login].tsx` (route `/profile/:login`) — the profile screen, fetches on mount via `useEffect`.

### Data layer

- `types/user42.ts` — Zod schema (`user42Schema`) validating the shape of `GET /v2/users/:login` responses at runtime (not just a compile-time type). Screens derive their state type via `z.infer<typeof user42Schema>` and call `user42Schema.parse(...)` on the parsed JSON before trusting it. Known API quirks the schema accounts for: `cursus_users` and `projects_users` are arrays (a user can have multiple cursus); `cursus_users[].grade` can be `null`; the projects_users field is literally named `"validated?"` (with the question mark in the key) — must be quoted as a string key both in the Zod schema and at every read site (`obj["validated?"]`, not `obj.validated?`, which parses as optional chaining).
- `lib/auth.ts` — `tokenStillValid()` (reads and re-parses stored token, checks `created_at + expires_in` against `Date.now()`, both need converting to the same unit — 42 sends seconds, `Date.now()` is ms) and `getAccessToken()` (extracts just the `access_token` string for the `Authorization` header). Both independently re-read/re-parse SecureStore — mildly redundant but functionally fine at this scale.

## 42 OAuth2 integration

Implemented and working end-to-end (dev build tested on iOS simulator): login screen → `useAuthRequest` → 42 authorize page → redirect via `swiftycompanion://callback` → code exchanged for token → token stored via `expo-secure-store` → `/search` and `/profile/[login]` call the real 42 API with `Authorization: Bearer`.

- The app's URL scheme is `swiftycompanion` (`app.json`); redirect URI registered on the intra app is `swiftycompanion://callback` (a bare `swiftycompanion://` was rejected by 42's app-registration form as "not an absolute URI" — needs a path segment after the scheme).
- Custom URL schemes only resolve to an installed app that has registered them in its own native bundle. Expo Go cannot receive this redirect (it owns the `exp://` scheme) — the OAuth flow requires the `expo-dev-client` dev build (`npx expo run:ios`), not Expo Go.
- **PKCE**: 42's web application flow guide documents only the classic Authorization Code exchange (`client_id` + `client_secret` + `code` + `redirect_uri` at `POST /oauth/token`, `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` body) — no PKCE support. `expo-auth-session`'s `useAuthRequest` enables PKCE by default, so the request config needs `usePKCE: false` explicitly. Decision made with the user: embed the `client_secret` in the app rather than standing up a proxy server — accepted trade-off, documented for defense (see below).
- The token exchange `redirect_uri` must be byte-for-byte identical to the one used in the authorization request (`useMemo`'d from the same `scheme`/`path` in `app/index.tsx`, never hardcoded twice) — 42 rejects the exchange otherwise.
- Env vars must be prefixed `EXPO_PUBLIC_` to be readable in client code via `process.env` (`EXPO_PUBLIC_API42_UID`, `EXPO_PUBLIC_API42_SECRET`) — an unprefixed var is invisible to the app bundle.
- Token response includes `access_token`, `refresh_token`, `expires_in` (seconds, relative), `created_at` (Unix seconds, absolute — used instead of a client-side timestamp to compute real expiry), and `secret_valid_until` (when the app's *client_secret* itself expires, unrelated to any single user's token lifetime — kept as a debug-only signal in `tokenStillValid()`, log-only, since it's virtually always much further out than `expires_in`).
- Do not create a new token per API query — reuse/cache the obtained access token (explicit subject requirement) and store it via `expo-secure-store`, not plain `AsyncStorage`.
- 42 rotates each app's client secret monthly — the `.env` value will need manual updates over the course of development. This also bounds the real-world exposure window of the embedded-secret trade-off (a leaked secret is only valid for ≤30 days), and doesn't scale to a real App Store release (would need a new build every ~30 days, or a proxy server) — both worth stating explicitly at defense.

## Not yet implemented

- Profile screen only reads `login` from the validated response — skills, projects, and the other required detail fields (email, phone, location, wallet, evaluations) still need to be wired into the UI.
- Bonus: refresh the access token via `refresh_token` on expiry (currently, an expired token just redirects back to `/` — no exclusive automatic refresh).

## Subject constraints

- At least 2 views; must support navigating back to the first.
- Must handle all error cases explicitly (login not found, network error, etc.) — no silent failures.
- Profile view requires the profile picture plus at least 4 details (e.g. login, email, mobile, level, location, wallet, evaluations).
- Must display skills with level and percentage, and completed projects including failed ones.
- Layout must use a flexible/constraint-based technique so the UI adapts across screen sizes.
- Bonus (not yet implemented): refresh the OAuth token on expiry, and the app must keep working in any case (network loss, expired token, etc.).
